   "xml_index": 2,
   "xml_name": "PS8_002_DEBUG Remove Short Words",
   "display_name": "Remove Short Words",
   "questiontext_html": "<p><b>This is a DEBUG-TYPE question. Please read this carefully, it works differently from the other questions.</b></p>\n<p>The answer box below already contains a complete function. For the one example test shown below, this function gives the correct output.</p>\n<p><b>But the function has a mistake</b> (possibly more than one). For some other valid inputs, which we have <b>not</b> shown you, it gives a wrong answer or stops with an error.</p>\n<p><b>What you need to do:</b></p>\n<ol><li>Read the code carefully and trace it in your head for different valid inputs, to find the inputs where it goes wrong.</li><li>Fix the mistake(s), so that the function works correctly for <b>all</b> valid inputs described below.</li><li>Add a short comment in the code saying which line(s) you changed and why.</li></ol>\n<p><b>How this question is checked:</b> this question has some <b>hidden tests</b> that check exactly those inputs. When you click <b>Check</b>, we run your code on the example test and on the hidden tests. The pre-filled code will fail at least one hidden test; once you have fixed the mistake(s) correctly, all tests will pass.</p>\n<hr>\n<p><b>The function:</b> <code>remove_short(words, n)</code> takes a list of strings <code>words</code> and an integer <code>n</code>, and <b>changes</b> the list <code>words</code> by removing <b>every</b> word with fewer than <code>n</code> letters. It returns nothing. The other words stay in their original order.</p>\n<p><b>Your fixed function must work for:</b> any list of strings (including an empty list) and any <code>n</code>.</p>\n<p><b>Example test (already passes):</b> after <code>words = [\"a\", \"tree\", \"is\", \"green\"]</code> and <code>remove_short(words, 3)</code>, <code>words</code> is <code>['tree', 'green']</code>.</p>",
   "answerpreload": "# The code below has a bug. It will not work correctly for some\n# valid inputs other than the one shown in the example.\n# Identify the BUG and CORRECT it. Add a comment saying what you changed.\n\ndef remove_short(words, n):\n    for w in words:\n        if len(w) < n:\n            words.remove(w)\n",
   "mark": 1.0,
   "tests": [
    {
     "testcode": "words = [\"a\", \"tree\", \"is\", \"green\"]\nremove_short(words, 3)\nprint(words)",
     "stdin": "",
     "expected": "['tree', 'green']",
     "extra": "",
     "show": true,
     "example": true,
     "in_precheck": true,
     "in_check": true
    },
    {
     "testcode": "words = [\"a\", \"is\", \"tree\"]\nremove_short(words, 3)\nprint(words)",
     "stdin": "",
     "expected": "['tree']",
     "extra": "",
     "show": false,
     "example": false,
     "in_precheck": false,
     "in_check": true
    },
    {
     "testcode": "words = [\"hi\", \"to\", \"go\", \"me\"]\nremove_short(words, 5)\nprint(words)",
     "stdin": "",
     "expected": "[]",
     "extra": "",
     "show": false,
     "example": false,
     "in_precheck": false,
     "in_check": true
    },
    {
     "testcode": "words = []\nremove_short(words, 2)\nprint(words)",
     "stdin": "",
     "expected": "[]",
     "extra": "",
     "show": false,
     "example": false,
     "in_precheck": false,
     "in_check": true
    },
    {
     "testcode": "words = [\"long\", \"word\"]\nremove_short(words, 1)\nprint(words)",
     "stdin": "",
     "expected": "['long', 'word']",
     "extra": "",
     "show": false,
     "example": false,
     "in_precheck": false,
     "in_check": true
    }
   ]
