  {
   "xml_index": 3,
   "xml_name": "PS8_003_DEBUG Even Numbers Collector",
   "display_name": "Even Numbers Collector",
   "questiontext_html": "<p><b>This is a DEBUG-TYPE question. Please read this carefully, it works differently from the other questions.</b></p>\n<p>The answer box below already contains a complete function. For the one example test shown below, this function gives the correct output.</p>\n<p><b>But the function has a mistake</b> (possibly more than one). For some other valid inputs, which we have <b>not</b> shown you, it gives a wrong answer or stops with an error.</p>\n<p><b>What you need to do:</b></p>\n<ol><li>Read the code carefully and trace it in your head for different valid inputs, to find the inputs where it goes wrong.</li><li>Fix the mistake(s), so that the function works correctly for <b>all</b> valid inputs described below.</li><li>Add a short comment in the code saying which line(s) you changed and why.</li></ol>\n<p><b>How this question is checked:</b> this question has some <b>hidden tests</b> that check exactly those inputs. When you click <b>Check</b>, we run your code on the example test and on the hidden tests. The pre-filled code will fail at least one hidden test; once you have fixed the mistake(s) correctly, all tests will pass.</p>\n<hr>\n<p><b>The function:</b> <code>evens(L)</code> takes a list of integers <code>L</code> and <b>returns</b> a new list with the even numbers of <code>L</code>, in their original order. Calling it several times must give the right answer <b>every</b> time: each call must only return the even numbers of <b>its own</b> <code>L</code>.</p>\n<p><b>Your fixed function must work for:</b> any list of integers, including an empty list, and any number of calls one after the other.</p>\n<p><b>Example test (already passes):</b> <code>evens([1, 2, 3, 4])</code> returns <code>[2, 4]</code>.</p>",
   "answerpreload": "# The code below has a bug. It will not work correctly for some\n# valid inputs other than the one shown in the example.\n# Identify the BUG and CORRECT it. Add a comment saying what you changed.\n\nresult = []\n\ndef evens(L):\n    for x in L:\n        if x % 2 == 0:\n            result.append(x)\n    return result\n",
   "mark": 1.0,
   "tests": [
    {
     "testcode": "print(evens([1, 2, 3, 4]))",
     "stdin": "",
     "expected": "[2, 4]",
     "extra": "",
     "show": true,
     "example": true,
     "in_precheck": true,
     "in_check": true
    },
    {
     "testcode": "print(evens([1, 2, 3, 4]))\nprint(evens([6, 7]))",
     "stdin": "",
     "expected": "[2, 4]\n[6]",
     "extra": "",
     "show": false,
     "example": false,
     "in_precheck": false,
     "in_check": true
    },
    {
     "testcode": "evens([2])\nprint(evens([]))",
     "stdin": "",
     "expected": "[]",
     "extra": "",
     "show": false,
     "example": false,
     "in_precheck": false,
     "in_check": true
    },
    {
     "testcode": "a = evens([4])\nb = evens([8])\nprint(a, b)",
     "stdin": "",
     "expected": "[4] [8]",
     "extra": "",
     "show": false,
     "example": false,
     "in_precheck": false,
     "in_check": true
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "xml_index": 4,
   "xml_name": "PS8_004_DEBUG A List Inside A Tuple",
   "display_name": "A List Inside A Tuple",
   "questiontext_html": "<p><b>This is a DEBUG-TYPE question. Please read this carefully, it works differently from the other questions.</b></p>\n<p>The answer box below already contains a complete function. For the one example test shown below, this function gives the correct output.</p>\n<p><b>But the function has a mistake</b> (possibly more than one). For some other valid inputs, which we have <b>not</b> shown you, it gives a wrong answer or stops with an error.</p>\n<p><b>What you need to do:</b></p>\n<ol><li>Read the code carefully and trace it in your head for different valid inputs, to find the inputs where it goes wrong.</li><li>Fix the mistake(s), so that the function works correctly for <b>all</b> valid inputs described below.</li><li>Add a short comment in the code saying which line(s) you changed and why.</li></ol>\n<p><b>How this question is checked:</b> this question has some <b>hidden tests</b> that check exactly those inputs. When you click <b>Check</b>, we run your code on the example test and on the hidden tests. The pre-filled code will fail at least one hidden test; once you have fixed the mistake(s) correctly, all tests will pass.</p>\n<hr>\n<p>A student's record is a tuple <code>(name, marks)</code>, where <code>marks</code> is a <b>list</b> of integers. The tuple itself cannot change, but the list <b>inside</b> it can!</p>\n<p><b>The function:</b> <code>with_mark(record, m)</code> takes such a record and an integer <code>m</code>, and <b>returns</b> a <b>new</b> record (a tuple) with the same name and a <b>new</b> list of marks: the old marks followed by <code>m</code>. The original record, including its list of marks, must <b>not</b> change.</p>\n<p><b>Your fixed function must work for:</b> any record, including one with an empty list of marks.</p>\n<p><b>Example test (already passes):</b> <code>with_mark((\"Asha\", [70, 85]), 90)</code> returns <code>('Asha', [70, 85, 90])</code>.</p>",
   "answerpreload": "# The code below has a bug. It will not work correctly for some\n# valid inputs other than the one shown in the example.\n# Identify the BUG and CORRECT it. Add a comment saying what you changed.\n\ndef with_mark(record, m):\n    name, marks = record\n    marks.append(m)\n    return (name, marks)\n",
   "mark": 1.0,
   "tests": [
    {
     "testcode": "print(with_mark((\"Asha\", [70, 85]), 90))",
     "stdin": "",
     "expected": "('Asha', [70, 85, 90])",
     "extra": "",
     "show": true,
     "example": true,
     "in_precheck": true,
     "in_check": true
    },
    {
     "testcode": "r = (\"Asha\", [70, 85])\nnew = with_mark(r, 90)\nprint(new)\nprint(r)",
     "stdin": "",
     "expected": "('Asha', [70, 85, 90])\n('Asha', [70, 85])",
     "extra": "",
     "show": false,
     "example": false,
     "in_precheck": false,
     "in_check": true
    },
    {
     "testcode": "r = (\"Ravi\", [])\nwith_mark(r, 50)\nwith_mark(r, 60)\nprint(r)",
     "stdin": "",
     "expected": "('Ravi', [])",
     "extra": "",
     "show": false,
     "example": false,
     "in_precheck": false,
     "in_check": true
    },
    {
     "testcode": "print(with_mark((\"Meera\", []), 1))",
     "stdin": "",
     "expected": "('Meera', [1])",
     "extra": "",
     "show": false,
     "example": false,
     "in_precheck": false,
     "in_check": true
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "xml_index": 5,
   "xml_name": "PS8_005_DEBUG Capitalise Every Word",
   "display_name": "Capitalise Every Word",
   "questiontext_html": "<p><b>This is a DEBUG-TYPE question. Please read this carefully, it works differently from the other questions.</b></p>\n<p>The answer box below already contains a complete function. For the one example test shown below, this function gives the correct output.</p>\n<p><b>But the function has a mistake</b> (possibly more than one). For some other valid inputs, which we have <b>not</b> shown you, it gives a wrong answer or stops with an error.</p>\n<p><b>What you need to do:</b></p>\n<ol><li>Read the code carefully and trace it in your head for different valid inputs, to find the inputs where it goes wrong.</li><li>Fix the mistake(s), so that the function works correctly for <b>all</b> valid inputs described below.</li><li>Add a short comment in the code saying which line(s) you changed and why.</li></ol>\n<p><b>How this question is checked:</b> this question has some <b>hidden tests</b> that check exactly those inputs. When you click <b>Check</b>, we run your code on the example test and on the hidden tests. The pre-filled code will fail at least one hidden test; once you have fixed the mistake(s) correctly, all tests will pass.</p>\n<hr>\n<p>Strings cannot be changed: <code>w.capitalize()</code> does not change <code>w</code>, it gives a <b>new</b> string (with the first letter capital and the rest small).</p>\n<p><b>The function:</b> <code>capitalise_all(words)</code> takes a list of strings <code>words</code> and <b>returns</b> a new list in which every word is capitalised, for example <code>\"goa\"</code> becomes <code>\"Goa\"</code>.</p>\n<p><b>Your fixed function must work for:</b> any list of words (small letters, capitals or mixed), including an empty list.</p>\n<p><b>Example test (already passes):</b> <code>capitalise_all([\"Goa\", \"Pune\"])</code> returns <code>['Goa', 'Pune']</code>.</p>",
   "answerpreload": "# The code below has a bug. It will not work correctly for some\n# valid inputs other than the one shown in the example.\n# Identify the BUG and CORRECT it. Add a comment saying what you changed.\n\ndef capitalise_all(words):\n    for w in words:\n        w = w.capitalize()\n    return words\n",
   "mark": 1.0,
   "tests": [
    {
     "testcode": "print(capitalise_all([\"Goa\", \"Pune\"]))",
     "stdin": "",
     "expected": "['Goa', 'Pune']",
     "extra": "",
     "show": true,
     "example": true,
     "in_precheck": true,
     "in_check": true
    },
    {
     "testcode": "print(capitalise_all([\"goa\", \"pune\"]))",
     "stdin": "",
     "expected": "['Goa', 'Pune']",
     "extra": "",
     "show": false,
     "example": false,
     "in_precheck": false,
     "in_check": true
    },
    {
     "testcode": "print(capitalise_all([\"dELHI\", \"Agra\", \"x\"]))",
     "stdin": "",
     "expected": "['Delhi', 'Agra', 'X']",
     "extra": "",
     "show": false,
     "example": false,
     "in_precheck": false,
     "in_check": true
    },
    {
     "testcode": "print(capitalise_all([]))",
     "stdin": "",
     "expected": "[]",
     "extra": "",
     "show": false,
     "example": false,
     "in_precheck": false,
     "in_check": true
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "xml_index": 6,
   "xml_name": "PS8_006_DEBUG Make A Grid",
   "display_name": "Make A Grid",
   "questiontext_html": "<p><b>This is a DEBUG-TYPE question. Please read this carefully, it works differently from the other questions.</b></p>\n<p>The answer box below already contains a complete function. For the one example test shown below, this function gives the correct output.</p>\n<p><b>But the function has a mistake</b> (possibly more than one). For some other valid inputs, which we have <b>not</b> shown you, it gives a wrong answer or stops with an error.</p>\n<p><b>What you need to do:</b></p>\n<ol><li>Read the code carefully and trace it in your head for different valid inputs, to find the inputs where it goes wrong.</li><li>Fix the mistake(s), so that the function works correctly for <b>all</b> valid inputs described below.</li><li>Add a short comment in the code saying which line(s) you changed and why.</li></ol>\n<p><b>How this question is checked:</b> this question has some <b>hidden tests</b> that check exactly those inputs. When you click <b>Check</b>, we run your code on the example test and on the hidden tests. The pre-filled code will fail at least one hidden test; once you have fixed the mistake(s) correctly, all tests will pass.</p>\n<hr>\n<p><b>The function:</b> <code>make_grid(rows, cols)</code> <b>returns</b> a new table (a list of <code>rows</code> lists), where every row is a list of <code>cols</code> zeros. Every row must be a <b>separate</b> list, so that changing one item, like <code>grid[0][1] = 5</code>, changes <b>only</b> that one item.</p>\n<p><b>Your fixed function must work for:</b> any <code>rows</code> and <code>cols</code> that are 1 or more.</p>\n<p><b>Example test (already passes):</b> <code>make_grid(2, 3)</code> returns <code>[[0, 0, 0], [0, 0, 0]]</code>.</p>",
   "answerpreload": "# The code below has a bug. It will not work correctly for some\n# valid inputs other than the one shown in the example.\n# Identify the BUG and CORRECT it. Add a comment saying what you changed.\n\ndef make_grid(rows, cols):\n    row = [0] * cols\n    return [row] * rows\n",
   "mark": 1.0,
   "tests": [
    {
     "testcode": "print(make_grid(2, 3))",
     "stdin": "",
     "expected": "[[0, 0, 0], [0, 0, 0]]",
     "extra": "",
     "show": true,
     "example": true,
     "in_precheck": true,
     "in_check": true
    },
    {
     "testcode": "g = make_grid(2, 3)\ng[0][1] = 5\nprint(g)",
     "stdin": "",
     "expected": "[[0, 5, 0], [0, 0, 0]]",
     "extra": "",
     "show": false,
     "example": false,
     "in_precheck": false,
     "in_check": true
    },
    {
     "testcode": "g = make_grid(3, 1)\ng[2][0] = 7\nprint(g)",
     "stdin": "",
     "expected": "[[0], [0], [7]]",
     "extra": "",
     "show": false,
     "example": false,
     "in_precheck": false,
     "in_check": true
    },
    {
     "testcode": "print(make_grid(1, 1))",
     "stdin": "",
     "expected": "[[0]]",
     "extra": "",
     "show": false,
     "example": false,
     "in_precheck": false,
     "in_check": true
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "xml_index": 7,
   "xml_name": "PS8_007_DEBUG Delete Several Positions",
   "display_name": "Delete Several Positions",
   "questiontext_html": "<p><b>This is a DEBUG-TYPE question. Please read this carefully, it works differently from the other questions.</b></p>\n<p>The answer box below already contains a complete function. For the one example test shown below, this function gives the correct output.</p>\n<p><b>But the function has a mistake</b> (possibly more than one). For some other valid inputs, which we have <b>not</b> shown you, it gives a wrong answer or stops with an error.</p>\n<p><b>What you need to do:</b></p>\n<ol><li>Read the code carefully and trace it in your head for different valid inputs, to find the inputs where it goes wrong.</li><li>Fix the mistake(s), so that the function works correctly for <b>all</b> valid inputs described below.</li><li>Add a short comment in the code saying which line(s) you changed and why.</li></ol>\n<p><b>How this question is checked:</b> this question has some <b>hidden tests</b> that check exactly those inputs. When you click <b>Check</b>, we run your code on the example test and on the hidden tests. The pre-filled code will fail at least one hidden test; once you have fixed the mistake(s) correctly, all tests will pass.</p>\n<hr>\n<p><b>The function:</b> <code>delete_at(L, positions)</code> takes a list <code>L</code> and a list of indexes <code>positions</code> (valid indexes of <code>L</code>, all different, sorted from smallest to largest). It <b>changes</b> <code>L</code> by deleting the items that were at those positions in the <b>original</b> list. It returns nothing.</p>\n<p><b>Your fixed function must work for:</b> any such <code>L</code> and <code>positions</code>, including an empty list of positions.</p>\n<p><b>Example test (already passes):</b> after <code>L = [10, 20, 30, 40]</code> and <code>delete_at(L, [1])</code>, <code>L</code> is <code>[10, 30, 40]</code>.</p>",
   "answerpreload": "# The code below has a bug. It will not work correctly for some\n# valid inputs other than the one shown in the example.\n# Identify the BUG and CORRECT it. Add a comment saying what you changed.\n\ndef delete_at(L, positions):\n    for p in positions:\n        del L[p]\n",
   "mark": 1.0,
   "tests": [
    {
     "testcode": "L = [10, 20, 30, 40]\ndelete_at(L, [1])\nprint(L)",
     "stdin": "",
     "expected": "[10, 30, 40]",
     "extra": "",
     "show": true,
     "example": true,
     "in_precheck": true,
     "in_check": true
    },
    {
     "testcode": "L = [10, 20, 30, 40]\ndelete_at(L, [0, 2])\nprint(L)",
     "stdin": "",
     "expected": "[20, 40]",
     "extra": "",
     "show": false,
     "example": false,
     "in_precheck": false,
     "in_check": true
    },
    {
     "testcode": "L = [\"a\", \"b\", \"c\"]\ndelete_at(L, [1, 2])\nprint(L)",
     "stdin": "",
     "expected": "['a']",
     "extra": "",
     "show": false,
     "example": false,
     "in_precheck": false,
     "in_check": true
    },
    {
     "testcode": "L = [1, 2]\ndelete_at(L, [])\nprint(L)",
     "stdin": "",
     "expected": "[1, 2]",
     "extra": "",
     "show": false,
     "example": false,
     "in_precheck": false,
     "in_check": true
