   "questiontext_html": "<p><b>This is a DEBUG-TYPE question. Please read this carefully, it works differently from the other questions.</b></p>\n<p>The answer box below already contains a complete function. For the one example test shown below, this function gives the correct output.</p>\n<p><b>But the function has a mistake</b> (possibly more than one). For some other valid inputs, which we have <b>not</b> shown you, it gives a wrong answer or stops with an error.</p>\n<p><b>What you need to do:</b></p>\n<ol><li>Read the code carefully and trace it in your head for different valid inputs, to find the inputs where it goes wrong.</li><li>Fix the mistake(s), so that the function works correctly for <b>all</b> valid inputs described below.</li><li>Add a short comment in the code saying which line(s) you changed and why.</li></ol>\n<p><b>How this question is checked:</b> this question has some <b>hidden tests</b> that check exactly those inputs. When you click <b>Check</b>, we run your code on the example test and on the hidden tests. The pre-filled code will fail at least one hidden test; once you have fixed the mistake(s) correctly, all tests will pass.</p>\n<hr>\n<p><b>The function:</b> <code>add_twice(L, x)</code> <b>changes</b> the list <code>L</code> by adding the value <code>x</code> <b>two times</b> at the end, and also <b>returns</b> <code>L</code>. The caller's own list must change: after <code>add_twice(L, x)</code>, the caller sees the two new items in <code>L</code>.</p>\n<p><b>Your fixed function must work for:</b> any list (including an empty list) and any value <code>x</code>.</p>\n<p><b>Example test (already passes):</b> <code>add_twice([1], 5)</code> returns <code>[1, 5, 5]</code>.</p>",
   "answerpreload": "# The code below has a bug. It will not work correctly for some\n# valid inputs other than the one shown in the example.\n# Identify the BUG and CORRECT it. Add a comment saying what you changed.\n\ndef add_twice(L, x):\n    L = L + [x, x]\n    return L\n",
