  {
   "xml_index": 12,
   "xml_name": "PS8_012_DEBUG A List Inside A Tuple",
   "display_name": "A List Inside A Tuple",
   "questiontext_html": "<p><b>This is a DEBUG-TYPE question. Please read this carefully, it works differently from the other questions.</b></p>\n<p>The answer box below already contains a complete function. For the one example test shown below, this function gives the correct output.</p>\n<p><b>But the function has a mistake</b> (possibly more than one). For some other valid inputs, which we have <b>not</b> shown you, it gives a wrong answer or stops with an error.</p>\n<p><b>What you need to do:</b></p>\n<ol><li>Read the code carefully and trace it in your head for different valid inputs, to find the inputs where it goes wrong.</li><li>Fix the mistake(s), so that the function works correctly for <b>all</b> valid inputs described below.</li><li>Add a short comment in the code saying which line(s) you changed and why.</li></ol>\n<p><b>How this question is checked:</b> this question has some <b>hidden tests</b> that check exactly those inputs. When you click <b>Check</b>, we run your code on the example test and on the hidden tests. The pre-filled code will fail at least one hidden test; once you have fixed the mistake(s) correctly, all tests will pass.</p>\n<hr>\n<p>A player's record is a tuple <code>(name, games)</code>, where <code>games</code> is a <b>list</b> of the games they play. The tuple itself cannot change, but the list <b>inside</b> it can!</p>\n<p><b>The function:</b> <code>with_game(record, g)</code> takes such a record and a game <code>g</code> (a string), and <b>returns</b> a <b>new</b> record (a tuple) with the same name and a <b>new</b> list of games: the old games followed by <code>g</code>. The original record, including its list of games, must <b>not</b> change.</p>\n<p><b>Your fixed function must work for:</b> any record, including one with an empty list of games.</p>\n<p><b>Example test (already passes):</b> <code>with_game((\"Kavya\", [\"chess\"]), \"tennis\")</code> returns <code>('Kavya', ['chess', 'tennis'])</code>.</p>",
   "answerpreload": "# The code below has a bug. It will not work correctly for some\n# valid inputs other than the one shown in the example.\n# Identify the BUG and CORRECT it. Add a comment saying what you changed.\n\ndef with_game(record, g):\n    name, games = record\n    games.append(g)\n    return (name, games)\n",
   "mark": 1.0,
   "tests": [
    {
     "testcode": "print(with_game((\"Kavya\", [\"chess\"]), \"tennis\"))",
     "stdin": "",
     "expected": "('Kavya', ['chess', 'tennis'])",
     "extra": "",
     "show": true,
     "example": true,
     "in_precheck": true,
     "in_check": true
    },
    {
     "testcode": "r = (\"Kavya\", [\"chess\"])\nnew = with_game(r, \"tennis\")\nprint(new)\nprint(r)",
     "stdin": "",
     "expected": "('Kavya', ['chess', 'tennis'])\n('Kavya', ['chess'])",
     "extra": "",
     "show": false,
     "example": false,
     "in_precheck": false,
     "in_check": true
    },
    {
     "testcode": "r = (\"Dev\", [])\nwith_game(r, \"golf\")\nwith_game(r, \"polo\")\nprint(r)",
     "stdin": "",
     "expected": "('Dev', [])",
     "extra": "",
     "show": false,
     "example": false,
     "in_precheck": false,
     "in_check": true
    },
    {
     "testcode": "print(with_game((\"Arjun\", []), \"kho-kho\"))",
     "stdin": "",
     "expected": "('Arjun', ['kho-kho'])",
     "extra": "",
     "show": false,
     "example": false,
     "in_precheck": false,
     "in_check": true
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "xml_index": 13,
   "xml_name": "PS8_013_DEBUG Clean Every Word",
   "display_name": "Clean Every Word",
   "questiontext_html": "<p><b>This is a DEBUG-TYPE question. Please read this carefully, it works differently from the other questions.</b></p>\n<p>The answer box below already contains a complete function. For the one example test shown below, this function gives the correct output.</p>\n<p><b>But the function has a mistake</b> (possibly more than one). For some other valid inputs, which we have <b>not</b> shown you, it gives a wrong answer or stops with an error.</p>\n<p><b>What you need to do:</b></p>\n<ol><li>Read the code carefully and trace it in your head for different valid inputs, to find the inputs where it goes wrong.</li><li>Fix the mistake(s), so that the function works correctly for <b>all</b> valid inputs described below.</li><li>Add a short comment in the code saying which line(s) you changed and why.</li></ol>\n<p><b>How this question is checked:</b> this question has some <b>hidden tests</b> that check exactly those inputs. When you click <b>Check</b>, we run your code on the example test and on the hidden tests. The pre-filled code will fail at least one hidden test; once you have fixed the mistake(s) correctly, all tests will pass.</p>\n<hr>\n<p>Strings cannot be changed: <code>w.strip()</code> does not change <code>w</code>, it gives a <b>new</b> string without the spaces at the start and at the end (for example <code>\"  mango \"</code> becomes <code>\"mango\"</code>).</p>\n<p><b>The function:</b> <code>clean_all(words)</code> takes a list of strings <code>words</code> and <b>returns</b> a new list in which the spaces at the start and end of every word have been removed.</p>\n<p><b>Your fixed function must work for:</b> any list of strings, including an empty list.</p>\n<p><b>Example test (already passes):</b> <code>clean_all([\"mango\", \"kiwi\"])</code> returns <code>['mango', 'kiwi']</code>.</p>",
   "answerpreload": "# The code below has a bug. It will not work correctly for some\n# valid inputs other than the one shown in the example.\n# Identify the BUG and CORRECT it. Add a comment saying what you changed.\n\ndef clean_all(words):\n    for w in words:\n        w = w.strip()\n    return words\n",
   "mark": 1.0,
   "tests": [
    {
     "testcode": "print(clean_all([\"mango\", \"kiwi\"]))",
     "stdin": "",
     "expected": "['mango', 'kiwi']",
     "extra": "",
     "show": true,
     "example": true,
     "in_precheck": true,
     "in_check": true
    },
    {
     "testcode": "print(clean_all([\"  mango \", \"kiwi  \"]))",
     "stdin": "",
     "expected": "['mango', 'kiwi']",
     "extra": "",
     "show": false,
     "example": false,
     "in_precheck": false,
     "in_check": true
    },
    {
     "testcode": "print(clean_all([\" a b \", \"c\"]))",
     "stdin": "",
     "expected": "['a b', 'c']",
     "extra": "",
     "show": false,
     "example": false,
     "in_precheck": false,
     "in_check": true
    },
    {
     "testcode": "print(clean_all([]))",
     "stdin": "",
     "expected": "[]",
     "extra": "",
     "show": false,
     "example": false,
     "in_precheck": false,
     "in_check": true
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "xml_index": 14,
   "xml_name": "PS8_014_DEBUG Make A Board",
   "display_name": "Make A Board",
   "questiontext_html": "<p><b>This is a DEBUG-TYPE question. Please read this carefully, it works differently from the other questions.</b></p>\n<p>The answer box below already contains a complete function. For the one example test shown below, this function gives the correct output.</p>\n<p><b>But the function has a mistake</b> (possibly more than one). For some other valid inputs, which we have <b>not</b> shown you, it gives a wrong answer or stops with an error.</p>\n<p><b>What you need to do:</b></p>\n<ol><li>Read the code carefully and trace it in your head for different valid inputs, to find the inputs where it goes wrong.</li><li>Fix the mistake(s), so that the function works correctly for <b>all</b> valid inputs described below.</li><li>Add a short comment in the code saying which line(s) you changed and why.</li></ol>\n<p><b>How this question is checked:</b> this question has some <b>hidden tests</b> that check exactly those inputs. When you click <b>Check</b>, we run your code on the example test and on the hidden tests. The pre-filled code will fail at least one hidden test; once you have fixed the mistake(s) correctly, all tests will pass.</p>\n<hr>\n<p><b>The function:</b> <code>make_board(n)</code> <b>returns</b> a new <code>n</code> by <code>n</code> board: a list of <code>n</code> rows, where every row is a list of <code>n</code> strings <code>\".\"</code>. Every row must be a <b>separate</b> list, so that placing a piece, like <code>board[1][0] = \"X\"</code>, changes <b>only</b> that one square.</p>\n<p><b>Your fixed function must work for:</b> any <code>n</code> that is 1 or more.</p>\n<p><b>Example test (already passes):</b> <code>make_board(2)</code> returns <code>[['.', '.'], ['.', '.']]</code>.</p>",
   "answerpreload": "# The code below has a bug. It will not work correctly for some\n# valid inputs other than the one shown in the example.\n# Identify the BUG and CORRECT it. Add a comment saying what you changed.\n\ndef make_board(n):\n    row = [\".\"] * n\n    return [row] * n\n",
   "mark": 1.0,
   "tests": [
    {
     "testcode": "print(make_board(2))",
     "stdin": "",
     "expected": "[['.', '.'], ['.', '.']]",
     "extra": "",
     "show": true,
     "example": true,
     "in_precheck": true,
     "in_check": true
    },
    {
     "testcode": "b = make_board(2)\nb[1][0] = \"X\"\nprint(b)",
     "stdin": "",
     "expected": "[['.', '.'], ['X', '.']]",
     "extra": "",
     "show": false,
     "example": false,
     "in_precheck": false,
     "in_check": true
    },
    {
     "testcode": "b = make_board(3)\nb[0][0] = \"O\"\nb[2][2] = \"X\"\nprint(b)",
     "stdin": "",
