   "xml_index": 15,
   "xml_name": "PS8_015_DEBUG Delete By Index In A Loop",
   "display_name": "Delete By Index In A Loop",
   "questiontext_html": "<p><b>This is a DEBUG-TYPE question. Please read this carefully, it works differently from the other questions.</b></p>\n<p>The answer box below already contains a complete function. For the one example test shown below, this function gives the correct output.</p>\n<p><b>But the function has a mistake</b> (possibly more than one). For some other valid inputs, which we have <b>not</b> shown you, it gives a wrong answer or stops with an error.</p>\n<p><b>What you need to do:</b></p>\n<ol><li>Read the code carefully and trace it in your head for different valid inputs, to find the inputs where it goes wrong.</li><li>Fix the mistake(s), so that the function works correctly for <b>all</b> valid inputs described below.</li><li>Add a short comment in the code saying which line(s) you changed and why.</li></ol>\n<p><b>How this question is checked:</b> this question has some <b>hidden tests</b> that check exactly those inputs. When you click <b>Check</b>, we run your code on the example test and on the hidden tests. The pre-filled code will fail at least one hidden test; once you have fixed the mistake(s) correctly, all tests will pass.</p>\n<hr>\n<p><b>The function:</b> <code>remove_value(L, x)</code> takes a list <code>L</code> and a value <code>x</code>, and <b>changes</b> <code>L</code> by removing <b>every</b> item equal to <code>x</code>. It returns nothing. The other items stay in their original order.</p>\n<p><b>Your fixed function must work for:</b> any list (including an empty list) and any value <code>x</code>.</p>\n<p><b>Example test (already passes):</b> after <code>L = [1, 2, 3]</code> and <code>remove_value(L, 3)</code>, <code>L</code> is <code>[1, 2]</code>.</p>",
   "answerpreload": "# The code below has a bug. It will not work correctly for some\n# valid inputs other than the one shown in the example.\n# Identify the BUG and CORRECT it. Add a comment saying what you changed.\n\ndef remove_value(L, x):\n    for i in range(len(L)):\n        if L[i] == x:\n            del L[i]\n",
   "mark": 1.0,
   "tests": [
    {
     "testcode": "L = [1, 2, 3]\nremove_value(L, 3)\nprint(L)",
     "stdin": "",
     "expected": "[1, 2]",
     "extra": "",
     "show": true,
     "example": true,
     "in_precheck": true,
     "in_check": true
    },
    {
     "testcode": "L = [3, 1, 3]\nremove_value(L, 3)\nprint(L)",
     "stdin": "",
     "expected": "[1]",
     "extra": "",
     "show": false,
     "example": false,
     "in_precheck": false,
     "in_check": true
    },
    {
     "testcode": "L = [2, 2, 5]\nremove_value(L, 2)\nprint(L)",
     "stdin": "",
     "expected": "[5]",
     "extra": "",
     "show": false,
     "example": false,
     "in_precheck": false,
     "in_check": true
    },
    {
     "testcode": "L = []\nremove_value(L, 1)\nprint(L)",
     "stdin": "",
     "expected": "[]",
     "extra": "",
     "show": false,
     "example": false,
     "in_precheck": false,
     "in_check": true
    },
    {
     "testcode": "L = [\"a\", \"b\"]\nremove_value(L, \"z\")\nprint(L)",
     "stdin": "",
     "expected": "['a', 'b']",
     "extra": "",
     "show": false,
     "example": false,
     "in_precheck": false,
     "in_check": true
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "xml_index": 16,
   "xml_name": "PS8_016_DEBUG Extend The List",
   "display_name": "Extend The List",
   "questiontext_html": "<p><b>This is a DEBUG-TYPE question. Please read this carefully, it works differently from the other questions.</b></p>\n<p>The answer box below already contains a complete function. For the one example test shown below, this function gives the correct output.</p>\n<p><b>But the function has a mistake</b> (possibly more than one). For some other valid inputs, which we have <b>not</b> shown you, it gives a wrong answer or stops with an error.</p>\n<p><b>What you need to do:</b></p>\n<ol><li>Read the code carefully and trace it in your head for different valid inputs, to find the inputs where it goes wrong.</li><li>Fix the mistake(s), so that the function works correctly for <b>all</b> valid inputs described below.</li><li>Add a short comment in the code saying which line(s) you changed and why.</li></ol>\n<p><b>How this question is checked:</b> this question has some <b>hidden tests</b> that check exactly those inputs. When you click <b>Check</b>, we run your code on the example test and on the hidden tests. The pre-filled code will fail at least one hidden test; once you have fixed the mistake(s) correctly, all tests will pass.</p>\n<hr>\n<p><b>The function:</b> <code>extend_with(L, M)</code> <b>changes</b> the list <code>L</code> by adding all the items of the list <code>M</code> at the end, and also <b>returns</b> <code>L</code>. The caller's own list must change: after <code>extend_with(L, M)</code>, the caller sees the new items in <code>L</code>.</p>\n<p><b>Your fixed function must work for:</b> any lists <code>L</code> and <code>M</code> (each may be empty).</p>\n<p><b>Example test (already passes):</b> <code>extend_with([1, 2], [3, 4])</code> returns <code>[1, 2, 3, 4]</code>.</p>",
   "answerpreload": "# The code below has a bug. It will not work correctly for some\n# valid inputs other than the one shown in the example.\n# Identify the BUG and CORRECT it. Add a comment saying what you changed.\n\ndef extend_with(L, M):\n    L = L + M\n    return L\n",
   "mark": 1.0,
   "tests": [
    {
     "testcode": "print(extend_with([1, 2], [3, 4]))",
     "stdin": "",
     "expected": "[1, 2, 3, 4]",
     "extra": "",
     "show": true,
     "example": true,
     "in_precheck": true,
     "in_check": true
    },
    {
     "testcode": "L = [1, 2]\nextend_with(L, [3, 4])\nprint(L)",
     "stdin": "",
     "expected": "[1, 2, 3, 4]",
     "extra": "",
     "show": false,
     "example": false,
     "in_precheck": false,
     "in_check": true
    },
    {
     "testcode": "L = []\nextend_with(L, [9])\nextend_with(L, [])\nprint(L)",
     "stdin": "",
     "expected": "[9]",
     "extra": "",
     "show": false,
     "example": false,
     "in_precheck": false,
     "in_check": true
    },
    {
     "testcode": "L = [0]\nN = extend_with(L, [1])\nprint(N is L)",
     "stdin": "",
     "expected": "True",
     "extra": "",
     "show": false,
     "example": false,
     "in_precheck": false,
     "in_check": true
    }
   ]
  }
